ICCV-2023-P1865,ICCV-2023-A009938,Jieneng Chen,shared,3,25,25,"DAMO Academy, Alibaba Group Johns Hopkins University Hupan Lab, Hangzhou, China Sun Yat-sen University Cancer Center The First Affiliated Hospital of Zhejiang University Shengjing Hospital of China Medical University Sichuan Cancer Hospital Guangdong Provincial People’s Hospital Guangdong Key Laboratory of Artificial Intelligence in Medical Image Analysis and Application Cancer Non-Cancer Lung Liver Hemangioma Colorectum Liver Metastasis Liver Stomach Lymphoma Stomach Esophagus leiomyoma Breast Pancreas MCN Esophagus Pancreas SCN Esophagus Lung Breast Stomach Liver Pancreas Kidney Colorectum 6th 7th Other cancer Pancreas 5th Kidney 4th Breast 3rd Esophagus Liver 2nd Stomach Lung Rank 1st Colorectum Cancer Mortality ( . million deaths in ) Pancreas … Human readers or radiologists routinely perform fullbody multi-organ multi-disease detection and diagnosis in clinical practice, while most medical AI systems are built to focus on single organs with a narrow list of a few diseases. This might severely limit AI’s clinical adoption. A certain number of AI models need to be assembled nontrivially to match the diagnostic process of a human reading a CT scan. In this paper, we construct a Unified Tumor Transformer (CancerUniT) model to jointly detect tumor existence & location and diagnose tumor characteristics for eight major cancers in CT scans. CancerUniT is a query-based Mask Transformer model with the output of multi-tumor prediction. We decouple the object queries into organ queries, tumor detection queries and tumor diagnosis queries, and further establish hierarchical relationships among the three groups. This clinically-inspired architecture effectively assists inter- and intra-organ representation learning of tumors and facilitates the resolution of these complex, anatomically related multi-organ cancer image reading tasks. CancerUniT is trained end-to-end using a curated large-scale CT images of , patients including eight major types of cancers and occurring non-cancer tumors (all are pathology-confirmed with 3D tumor masks annotated by radiologists). On the test set of patients, CancerUniT has demonstrated strong performance under a set of clinically relevant evaluation metrics, substantially outperforming both multi-disease methods and an assembly of eight single-organ expert models in tumor detection, Tumor Segmentation & Diagnosis Cancer Detection in Organs",-9,data/raw/iccv/2023/first_pages/ICCV-2023-P1865.txt,https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/ICCV2023/papers/Chen_CancerUniT_Towards_a_Single_Unified_Model_for_Effective_Detection_Segmentation_ICCV_2023_paper.pdf
